1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:03,360
Why make a film about Ned Kelly?
为什么要拍一部关于内德·凯利的电影？

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:10,050
More ingenious crimes than those committed by the reckless Australian bandit are reported every day.
每天见诸报道的罪案中，都有比这个鲁莽的澳大利亚盗匪所犯罪行更巧妙的。

3
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:18,940
What is there in Ned Kelly to justify dragging the mesmeric Mick Jagger so far into the Australian bush and away from his natural haunts?
内德·凯利身上有什么，值得把摄人心魄的米克·贾格尔大老远拖进澳大利亚丛林、让他远离平日出没的地方？

4
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:30,240
The answer is that the film makers know we always fall for a bandit, and Jagger is set to do for bold Ned Kelly what Brando once did for the arrogant Emiliano Zapata.
答案是：电影制作人知道我们总会迷上盗匪；白兰度当年为傲慢的埃米利亚诺·萨帕塔所做的，贾格尔也将为大胆的内德·凯利再做一次。

5
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:44,380
The bandit inhabits a special realm of legend where his deeds are embroidered by others; where his death rather than his life is considered beyond belief; where the men who bring him to “justice” are afflicted with doubts about their role.
盗匪栖身于一个特殊的传奇国度：在那里，他的事迹经别人添枝加叶；在那里，人们觉得难以置信的不是他的一生，而是他的死；在那里，将他“绳之以法”的人，会为自己所扮演的角色饱受疑虑困扰。

6
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:49,720
The bandits had a role to play as definite as that of the authorities who condemned them.
盗匪要扮演的角色，和给他们定罪的当局所扮演的角色一样明确。

7
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:58,550
These were men in conflict with authority, and, in the absence of strong law or the idea of loyal opposition, they took to the hills.
这些人与当局相冲突；在既没有强有力的法律、也没有忠诚反对派这种观念的情况下，他们便遁入了山林。

8
00:00:59,350 --> 00:01:03,280
Even there, however, many of them obeyed certain unwritten rules.
然而，即便在山里，他们中的许多人仍遵守着某些不成文的规矩。

9
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:13,810
These robbers, who claimed to be something more than mere thieves, had in common, firstly, a sense of loyalty to and identity with the peasants they came from.
这些强盗自称不只是区区小偷，他们的共同之处，首先是对自己出身的农民怀有忠诚感和认同感。

10
00:01:14,610 --> 00:01:18,180
They didn’t steal the peasant’s harvest; they did steal the lord’s.
他们不偷农民的收成，领主的收成却照偷不误。

11
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:24,470
And certain characteristics seem to apply to “social bandits” whether they were in Sicily or Peru.
而且，无论是在西西里还是在秘鲁，某些特征似乎都适用于“社会盗匪”。

12
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:30,590
They were generally young men under the age of marriage, predictably the best age for dissidence.
他们一般是未到婚龄的年轻男子，可想而知，这是最适合反叛的年纪。

13
00:01:31,390 --> 00:01:47,060
Some were simply the surplus male population who had to look for another source of income; others were runaway serfs or ex-soldiers; a minority, though the most interesting, were outstanding men who were unwilling to accept the meek and passive role of peasant.
有些人只不过是过剩的男性人口，不得不另谋收入来源；另一些人是逃亡的农奴或退伍士兵；还有少数人——却也是最有意思的一类——是些出众的人物，不甘接受农民那种逆来顺受、消极被动的角色。

14
00:01:47,860 --> 00:01:55,640
They usually operated in bands between ten and twenty strong and relied for survival on difficult terrain and bad transport.
他们通常结成十到二十人的团伙活动，靠地形险恶、交通不便来求生存。

15
00:01:56,440 --> 00:02:02,150
And bandits prospered best where authority was merely local—over the next hill and they were free.
而在当局的权力仅及于本地的地区，盗匪最为兴旺——翻过下一座山，他们就自由了。

16
00:02:02,950 --> 00:02:12,170
Unlike the general run of peasantry they had a taste for flamboyant dress and gesture; but they usually shared the peasants’ religious beliefs and superstitions.
与一般农民不同，他们喜爱张扬的衣着和举止；不过，他们通常与农民有着相同的宗教信仰和迷信观念。

17
00:02:12,970 --> 00:02:25,070
The first sign of a man caught up in the Robin Hood syndrome was when he started out, forced into outlawry as a victim of injustice; and when he then set out to “right wrongs”, first his own and then other people’s.
一个人陷入罗宾汉综合征，最初的迹象是：起步时，他身为不公的受害者，被迫沦为法外之徒；随后，他着手“讨回公道”，先为自己，再为他人。

18
00:02:25,870 --> 00:02:50,040
The classic bandit then “takes from the rich and gives to the poor” in conformity with his own sense of social justice; he never kills except in self-defense or justifiable revenge; his people admire and help to protect him; he dies through the treason of one of them; he behaves as if invisible and invulnerable; he is a “loyalist”, never the enemy of the king but only of the local oppressors.
典型的盗匪随后会依照自己的社会正义观“劫富济贫”；除非出于自卫或正当的复仇，他从不杀人；他的乡亲钦佩他，并帮着保护他；他死于某个乡亲的背叛；他行事仿佛自己能隐身、刀枪不入；他是“忠君者”，从不与国王为敌，只与地方上的压迫者为敌。

19
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:58,210
None of the bandits lived up fully to this image of the “noble robber” and for many the claim of larger motives was often a delusion.
没有哪个盗匪完全符合这种“侠盗”形象；对许多盗匪来说，自称怀有更远大的动机，往往只是他们自己的妄想。

20
00:02:59,010 --> 00:03:06,160
Yet amazingly, many of these violent men did behave at least half the time in accordance with this idealist pattern.
然而令人惊讶的是，这些暴戾之徒中有许多人确实至少有一半时间是按照这种理想主义模式行事的。

21
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:13,250
Pancho Villa in Mexico and Salvatore Giuliano in Italy began their careers harshly victimized.
墨西哥的潘乔·比利亚和意大利的萨尔瓦托雷·朱利亚诺，在生涯之初都曾惨遭迫害。

22
00:03:14,050 --> 00:03:17,000
Many of their charitable acts later became legends.
他们的许多善举后来都成了传奇。

23
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:25,390
Far from being defeated by death, bandits’ reputation for invincibility was often strengthened by the manner of their dying.
盗匪不可战胜的名声非但没有败给死亡，反而常常因他们的死法而更加牢固。

24
00:03:26,190 --> 00:03:34,680
The “dirty little coward” who shot Jesse James in the back is in every ballad about him, and the implication is that nothing else could have brought Jesse down.
从背后向杰西·詹姆斯开枪的那个“卑鄙的小懦夫”，出现在每一首唱他的民谣里，言下之意是：除此之外，再没有什么能把杰西打倒。

25
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:42,770
Even when the police claimed the credit, as they tried to do at first with Giuliano’s death, the local people refused to believe it.
即便警方把功劳揽到自己头上——朱利亚诺死后，警方起初就曾试图这么做——当地百姓也拒绝相信。

26
00:03:43,570 --> 00:03:52,410
And it is not just the bandit’s vitality that prompts the people to refuse to believe that their hero has died; his death would be in some way the death of hope.
而且，促使百姓拒绝相信自己的英雄已死的，不只是盗匪的生命力；他要是死了，在某种意义上就等于希望也死了。

27
00:03:53,210 --> 00:04:00,720
For the traditional “noble robber” represents an extremely primitive form of social protest, perhaps the most primitive there is.
因为传统的“侠盗”代表着一种极其原始的社会抗议形式，或许是所有形式中最原始的一种。

28
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:06,030
He is an individual who refuses to bend his back, that is all.
他是一个拒绝折腰的个人，仅此而已。

29
00:04:06,830 --> 00:04:12,700
Most protesters will eventually be bought over and persuaded to come to terms with the official power.
大多数抗议者最终都会被收买，被说服与官方权力妥协。

30
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:23,170
That is why the few who do not, or who are believed to have remained uncontaminated, have so great and passionate a burden of admiration and longing laid upon them.
正因如此，那少数没有就范的人，或者被认为始终未受玷污的人，才会背负着如此沉重而炽热的钦佩与渴盼。

31
00:04:23,970 --> 00:04:25,600
They cannot abolish oppression.
他们无法消除压迫。

32
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:31,930
But they do prove that justice is possible, that poor men need not be humble, helpless and meek.
但他们确实证明了：正义是可以实现的，穷人不必卑微、无助、逆来顺受。

33
00:04:32,730 --> 00:04:40,890
The bandit in the real world is rooted in peasant society and when its simple agricultural system is left behind, so is he.
现实世界中的盗匪植根于农民社会；一旦这种社会的简单农业体系成为过去，盗匪也就随之成为过去。

34
00:04:41,690 --> 00:04:48,400
But the tales and legends, the books and films continue to appear for an audience that is neither peasant nor bandit.
但那些故事和传说、书籍和电影仍在不断问世，面向的受众既不是农民，也不是盗匪。

35
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:59,860
In some ways the characters and deeds of the great bandits could so readily be the stuff of grand opera—Don Jose in “Carmen” is based on the Andalusian bandit El Tempranillo.
在某些方面，那些著名盗匪的性格和事迹很容易就能成为大歌剧的素材——《卡门》中的唐·何塞，就是以安达卢西亚盗匪埃尔·坦普拉尼略为原型的。

36
00:05:00,660 --> 00:05:06,550
But they are perhaps more at home in folk songs, in popular tales and in the ritual dramas of films.
但它们放在民歌、通俗故事和电影这种仪式化的戏剧里，或许更为适得其所。

37
00:05:07,350 --> 00:05:22,610
When we sit in the darkness of the cinema to watch the bold deeds of bandits like Ned Kelly we are caught up in admiration for their strong individuality, their simple gesture of protest, their passion for justice and their confidence that they cannot be beaten.
当我们坐在漆黑的电影院里，观看内德·凯利这类盗匪的大胆事迹时，会沉浸在对他们的钦佩之中：钦佩他们鲜明的个性、简单的抗议姿态、对正义的热忱，以及认定自己不会被打败的那份自信。

38
00:05:23,410 --> 00:05:28,300
This sustains us nearly as much as it did the almost hopeless people from whom they sprang.
这带给我们的支撑，几乎不亚于当年带给那些近乎绝望的百姓的支撑——盗匪正是从这些百姓中走出来的。
